Reports pointer arithmetic that converts an 'unsafe.Pointer' to 'uintptr', adds an offset, and converts the result back to 'unsafe.Pointer'. This pattern can be unsafe because the 'uintptr' value can be observed by the garbage collector between the conversion and the next pointer use. In Go 1.17 and later, 'unsafe.Add' expresses the same operation directly. Example: 'func Offset(p unsafe.Pointer, n int) unsafe.Pointer {
	return unsafe.Pointer(uintptr(p) + uintptr(n))
}' To fix the code, use the Simplify pointer addition using unsafe.Add quick-fix. After the quick-fix is applied: 'func Offset(p unsafe.Pointer, n int) unsafe.Pointer {
	return unsafe.Add(p, n)
}' Inspection ID: GoFixUnsafeFuncs

